# Output format

Output two top-level blocks in this order: `<introduction>` and
`<screens>`. The `<introduction>` is optional — a short prose preamble
naming which slugs came from which journeys and any standalone
screens you added.

After `<introduction>`, output a single `<screens>` block. Inside it,
each `<screen>` has this shape — `<slug>`, `<purpose>`, `<feats>`,
`<states>`, `<affordances>`, `<displayed-data>`, an optional
`<screen-group>`, an optional `<journeys>` block, and an optional
`<navigation>` block, in this order:

    <introduction>
    plan-select, payment-details, and subscribe-confirm come from
    the "new customer subscribes" journey; invoice-list and
    invoice-detail come from "customer reviews past invoices".
    Added one standalone screen, account-settings, for profile and
    notification preferences — no journey names it.
    </introduction>
    <screens>
      <screen>
        <slug>plan-select</slug>
        <purpose>Let a visitor compare plans and pick one to start
        subscribing.</purpose>
        <feats>
          <feat id="feat_signup01"/>
        </feats>
        <states>
          <state name="default"/>
          <state name="loading"/>
          <state name="error"/>
        </states>
        <affordances>
          <affordance>
            <action>select a plan</action>
            <intent>advance to payment-details with the chosen plan
            carried forward</intent>
            <data-out>plan id, billing cycle</data-out>
          </affordance>
        </affordances>
        <displayed-data>Plan name, price per cycle, feature comparison
        grid.</displayed-data>
        <journeys>
          <journey ref="journey_signup01"/>
        </journeys>
        <navigation>
          <edge to="payment-details"/>
        </navigation>
      </screen>
      <screen>
        <slug>account-settings</slug>
        <purpose>Let a signed-in user edit their profile and
        notification preferences.</purpose>
        <feats>
          <feat id="feat_profile04"/>
        </feats>
        <states>
          <state name="default"/>
          <state name="saving"/>
          <state name="saved"/>
          <state name="error"/>
        </states>
        <affordances>
          <affordance>
            <action>save changes</action>
            <intent>persist the edited profile fields</intent>
            <data-in>display name, email, notification toggles</data-in>
          </affordance>
        </affordances>
        <displayed-data>Current profile fields and notification
        preferences.</displayed-data>
        <screen-group>account</screen-group>
      </screen>
    </screens>

# Rules

## Structure

* Each `<screen>` has exactly one `<slug>`, `<purpose>`, `<feats>`,
  `<states>`, `<affordances>`, `<displayed-data>`, at most one
  `<screen-group>`, at most one `<journeys>`, at most one
  `<navigation>`. No other tags.
* **`<slug>` is the spine** — it derives the `screen:<slug>` mutex
  label, the file map, the component module, and the story file
  (later phases; not this ticket's concern, but the reason the slug
  has to be right the first time). Short, kebab-case, project-specific
  (`plan-select`, not `screen1` or `page`). Unique across the whole
  `<screens>` block. When a journey's walk already named this slug,
  reuse it verbatim — do not rename.

## Feats, states, affordances, displayed data

* `<feats>` may be empty (`<feats/>`) for a screen manifesting no
  feature directly — rare, but legal for pure plumbing surfaces.
* **`<states>` is a named state list, and the names are storybook
  variation names** — decided once, here, and inherited verbatim by
  the delivery-time screen doc, the stories, the CI variation audit,
  and reconciliation. Name every materially distinct rendering: at
  minimum the default; almost always `loading` and `error` for any
  screen that fetches or submits; add narrower names
  (`empty`, `denied`, `saving`) whenever the screen's behavior at that
  state is genuinely different from the ones already named. Don't
  invent a state with nothing distinct to show.
* **`<affordances>` is the contract check** — reconciliation verifies
  the landed screen exposes every affordance declared here; the audit
  verifies each maps to a reachable backend export and flags a
  capability with no surface or a surface with no capability. Each
  `<affordance>` names one user action: `<action>` (a short verb
  phrase), `<intent>` (what happens and why, one sentence),
  `<data-in>` (what the user supplies, omit if none), `<data-out>`
  (what the action produces or carries forward, omit if none). One
  affordance per distinct action — a screen with a form submit and a
  cancel button has two affordances, not one that describes both.
* `<displayed-data>` is one to three sentences naming what the screen
  shows, specific enough that a later design pass knows what layout
  has to accommodate. "Shows the user's data" is not specific;
  "invoice number, issue date, line items, running total, payment
  status badge" is.

## Journeys and navigation

* `<journeys>` is the formal record of which journeys this screen
  belongs to — zero or more `<journey ref="..."/>` children, each
  matching a journey `id` from the input list above exactly. Empty or
  omitted `<journeys>` means a standalone screen (settings, an about
  page) — legal and expected, not a gap. A screen named by more than
  one journey's walk gets one `<journey ref="..."/>` per journey that
  names it, not a separate screen per journey.
* `<navigation>` is zero or more `<edge to="..."/>` children, each
  naming another screen's `<slug>` in this same `<screens>` block —
  the ordinary paths a user can take from this screen (a submit that
  advances, a back link, a nav-bar destination), not limited to a
  journey's own linear walk. **Navigation edges are cyclic and never
  gate anything** — two screens linking back and forth is the normal
  case, not an error; do not avoid a back-edge to keep the graph
  acyclic, there is no such requirement here. Omit `<navigation>`
  entirely for a screen with no outgoing links you can name yet
  (rare — most screens link somewhere).

## Coverage

* Every screen slug that appears in any journey's ordered walk (the
  input list above) must get exactly one `<screen>` definition here.
  A slug with no definition, or two definitions sharing a slug, is a
  structural error.
* You may add standalone screens no journey walks. You may not add a
  screen that duplicates one a journey already names under a
  different slug — reuse the journey's slug.

## Meta-rules

* Do not include commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the list. Output only the `<screens>` block.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` inside prose text are fine — the parser
  tolerates them.
